1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,313
Sir Manasseh Meyer:

2
00:00:01,393 --> 00:00:05,425
Singapore's Jewish Landlord
and the Synagogues He Built

3
00:00:05,425 --> 00:00:10,294
Sir Manasseh Meyer arrived in
Singapore from Calcutta as a

4
00:00:10,374 --> 00:00:15,585
boy of 15 in 1861 and died at
his house on Oxley Rise in 1930,

5
00:00:15,665 --> 00:00:20,700
reputed to be a millionaire in
sterling several times over.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,818
In between he built a trading
firm, became one of the largest

7
00:00:24,898 --> 00:00:28,603
property owners in the colony,
and paid for both of the

8
00:00:28,683 --> 00:00:32,950
synagogues that Singapore's
Jewish community still uses today.

9
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:38,085
His hotels, flats and shophouses have
almost all gone, but the synagogues,

10
00:00:38,165 --> 00:00:41,900
a college building and a
school still carry his name.

11
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:47,822
Manasseh Meyer was born in Baghdad in
1846 and went to school in Calcutta,

12
00:00:47,902 --> 00:00:52,525
where many Baghdadi Jewish
families had settled to trade.

13
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:56,386
In 1861 he came to
Singapore, where his uncle,

14
00:00:56,466 --> 00:01:01,350
Joshua Raphael Joshua, ran a
firm called Joshua Brothers.

15
00:01:01,350 --> 00:01:06,024
He continued his education at
Saint Joseph's Institution,

16
00:01:06,104 --> 00:01:10,275
which at the time had a
single class of 25 pupils.

17
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:13,808
Three years later he went
back to Calcutta to learn

18
00:01:13,888 --> 00:01:17,925
book-keeping and Hebrew in
his maternal uncle's business.

19
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:22,396
In 1867, at 21, he set
up on his own in Rangoon,

20
00:01:22,476 --> 00:01:25,700
and he stayed there for six years.

21
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:29,560
In 1873 he returned to
Singapore and founded

22
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:33,400
an import and export firm, Meyer Brothers.

23
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,647
It grew into the largest local
firm in the trade with India and,

24
00:01:37,727 --> 00:01:42,325
according to his obituary, one of
the most profitable in the colony.

25
00:01:42,325 --> 00:01:46,016
It also dealt in opium,
which was then a legal trade:

26
00:01:46,096 --> 00:01:52,278
the Straits Times recorded that before the
import of opium became a government monopoly,

27
00:01:52,358 --> 00:01:56,200
his firm "handled large
quantities of that commodity".

28
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,801
Meyer began buying property in 1885,

29
00:01:59,881 --> 00:02:05,300
and his largest purchases
came between 1890 and 1892.

30
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:09,224
In 1890 he bought Killiney
House on Oxley Rise,

31
00:02:09,304 --> 00:02:13,653
built in 1842 for the
colonial surgeon Thomas Oxley,

32
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:16,800
rebuilt it and renamed it Belle Vue.

33
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:20,725
It became his family home
for the rest of his life.

34
00:02:20,725 --> 00:02:24,775
His holdings were spread
across the town and beyond it.

35
00:02:24,775 --> 00:02:27,799
He owned much of the
property around Raffles

36
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:31,045
Place and Collyer Quay,
and when he died a new

37
00:02:31,125 --> 00:02:34,300
Meyer Building was going
up in Raffles Place.

38
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:37,746
He built the Adelphi Hotel
on Coleman Street and

39
00:02:37,826 --> 00:02:41,425
Meyer Mansions opposite
Saint Andrew's Cathedral.

40
00:02:41,425 --> 00:02:45,139
At Tanjong Katong he built
the Sea View Hotel and,

41
00:02:45,219 --> 00:02:48,781
in 1909, Crescent Flats,
which the Straits Times

42
00:02:48,861 --> 00:02:52,275
called the first block
of flats in Singapore.

43
00:02:52,275 --> 00:02:56,562
Meyer Road in Katong was
named after him in 1921,

44
00:02:56,642 --> 00:03:02,525
and the story of that seafront is
told in the Tanjong Katong post.

45
00:03:02,525 --> 00:03:07,622
From 1893 to 1900 he sat on the
Municipal Commission, the body that

46
00:03:07,702 --> 00:03:12,722
ran the town's roads, water and
sanitation, where his knowledge of

47
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:17,825
property and local conditions was
described as especially useful.

48
00:03:17,825 --> 00:03:21,387
In 1911 he led a successful
protest against a Bill

49
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:25,400
that would have abolished
the Municipal Commissioners.

50
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:29,550
He also served on the Straits
Committee on Currency.

51
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:35,007
Singapore's first synagogue was a
small building on Synagogue Street,

52
00:03:35,087 --> 00:03:39,100
put up in the 1840s with
room for 30 or 40 people.

53
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:43,571
When Meyer came back in 1873 he
found it run-down and the street

54
00:03:43,651 --> 00:03:47,552
around it overcrowded, so he
asked the Attorney-General,

55
00:03:47,632 --> 00:03:52,325
Thomas Braddell, to let the community
sell it and build elsewhere.

56
00:03:52,325 --> 00:03:57,146
The government agreed, and a new
synagogue, Maghain Aboth ("Shield

57
00:03:57,226 --> 00:04:02,350
of Our Fathers"), opened on Waterloo
Street on the 4th of April 1878.

58
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:04,745
Meyer later paid for galleries so

59
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:07,600
that women could attend the services.

60
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:11,992
By the early 1900s the
community had more than doubled,

61
00:04:12,072 --> 00:04:16,950
to about 500 people, and Maghain
Aboth was becoming crowded.

62
00:04:16,950 --> 00:04:20,624
Meyer was also troubled by
frequent disagreements among

63
00:04:20,704 --> 00:04:24,800
members of different backgrounds
over the order of services.

64
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,339
In 1902 he decided to
build a private synagogue

65
00:04:28,419 --> 00:04:31,343
of his own in the grounds of Belle Vue,

66
00:04:31,423 --> 00:04:35,350
and held services in a
hired house in the meantime.

67
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:39,721
Chesed-El, meaning "bountiful
mercy and goodness of God",

68
00:04:39,801 --> 00:04:42,767
was designed by R. A. J. Bidwell of the

69
00:04:42,847 --> 00:04:47,999
architects Swan & Maclaren and
dedicated on the 14th of April 1905,

70
00:04:48,079 --> 00:04:51,125
with Meyer leading the service himself.

71
00:04:51,125 --> 00:04:54,350
The building carries his
initial: an "M" for Meyer

72
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:58,713
appears in the mouldings and on
the railings of the upper gallery,

73
00:04:58,793 --> 00:05:00,975
which is unusual for a synagogue.

74
00:05:00,975 --> 00:05:03,647
A chair with a small plaque bearing his

75
00:05:03,727 --> 00:05:06,550
name marks where he sat with his family.

76
00:05:06,550 --> 00:05:10,479
He paid poor men from the
community to make up the minyan,

77
00:05:10,559 --> 00:05:13,036
the ten men needed for daily prayers,

78
00:05:13,116 --> 00:05:15,950
so that services could be held every day.

79
00:05:15,950 --> 00:05:20,300
He also kept his own Hebrew
school on Bencoolen Street.

80
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:25,075
Meyer gave to causes inside
and outside his community.

81
00:05:25,075 --> 00:05:29,665
His largest gift was $150,000
for a science block at

82
00:05:29,745 --> 00:05:33,797
the new Raffles College,
which opened in 1928,

83
00:05:33,877 --> 00:05:39,625
and during the First World War
he gave $20,000 for an aeroplane.

84
00:05:39,625 --> 00:05:43,903
On the 2nd of November 1922
about 300 guests gathered

85
00:05:43,983 --> 00:05:48,179
at Belle Vue to hear Albert
Einstein, who was on his

86
00:05:48,259 --> 00:05:52,619
way to Japan and had been
asked to raise money for the

87
00:05:52,699 --> 00:05:56,975
new Hebrew University in
Jerusalem; Meyer gave £500.

88
00:05:56,975 --> 00:06:00,831
He also promised an organ
for a concert hall that the

89
00:06:00,911 --> 00:06:05,287
Philharmonic Society hoped to
build, but the First World War

90
00:06:05,367 --> 00:06:09,600
intervened and the hall had
still not been begun in 1921.

91
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:13,042
He avoided publicity,
and he did not appear at

92
00:06:13,122 --> 00:06:16,950
all in the "Who's Who in
Malaya" of the mid-1920s.

93
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:20,555
In March 1929 he was made
a Knight Bachelor "for

94
00:06:20,635 --> 00:06:24,316
public services and
benevolence", the only honour

95
00:06:24,396 --> 00:06:27,850
given to Malaya in that
year's New Year list.

96
00:06:27,850 --> 00:06:30,966
By then, the Straits
Times noted, he had taken

97
00:06:31,046 --> 00:06:34,093
little part in public
life for some years and

98
00:06:34,173 --> 00:06:37,300
was not well known to
the younger generation.

99
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:42,673
Sir Manasseh Meyer died at Belle
Vue on the 1st of July 1930,

100
00:06:42,753 --> 00:06:46,150
aged 84, after an illness of ten days.

101
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:49,574
After a short service at
Chesed-El he was buried

102
00:06:49,654 --> 00:06:53,450
that morning at the Jewish
cemetery on Thomson Road.

103
00:06:53,450 --> 00:06:56,825
He left three sons and four daughters.

104
00:06:56,825 --> 00:06:59,589
His will made his three sons, Isaac,

105
00:06:59,669 --> 00:07:03,225
Jacob and Reuben, his
executors and trustees.

106
00:07:03,225 --> 00:07:07,286
It kept Chesed-El and the Hebrew
school going with the rents

107
00:07:07,366 --> 00:07:11,290
of six houses in front of the
school, and set aside houses

108
00:07:11,370 --> 00:07:15,017
in Cecil Street, Telok Ayer
Street and Japan Street to

109
00:07:15,097 --> 00:07:19,100
fund charities in Jerusalem
and for Jewish people in need.

110
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:24,475
During the Japanese Occupation no
services were held at Chesed-El.

111
00:07:24,475 --> 00:07:28,245
The Japanese military used
the building to store heavy

112
00:07:28,325 --> 00:07:32,175
goods and ammunition, and
took out its wooden benches.

113
00:07:32,175 --> 00:07:37,506
Singapore's Jewish community had
grown to nearly 2,000 before the war,

114
00:07:37,586 --> 00:07:41,139
but about half left as
the Japanese approached,

115
00:07:41,219 --> 00:07:46,475
and on the 5th of April 1943 more
than 100 Jewish men were interned.

116
00:07:46,475 --> 00:07:51,500
Fewer than 700 Jewish people
remained when the war ended.

117
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:54,825
After the war the estate was wound up.

118
00:07:54,825 --> 00:07:59,477
In December 1947 the Straits
Times reported that it would sell

119
00:07:59,557 --> 00:08:04,057
shops at Nos. 4 and 5 Raffles
Place, Meyer Mansions on North

120
00:08:04,137 --> 00:08:08,560
Bridge Road and large plots of
freehold land at Paya Lebar,

121
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:13,525
East Coast Road, Thomson Road,
Dunearn Road and Kheam Hock Road.

122
00:08:13,525 --> 00:08:17,531
In December 1949 a block of
Meyer family property worth

123
00:08:17,611 --> 00:08:21,766
about six million dollars was
offered for sale by tender,

124
00:08:21,846 --> 00:08:25,481
the first time since the
war that so many valuable

125
00:08:25,561 --> 00:08:29,425
freehold lands and houses
had been offered together.

126
00:08:29,425 --> 00:08:33,146
At an auction reported on
the 1st of December 1950,

127
00:08:33,226 --> 00:08:36,351
land and houses worth
more than $2 million,

128
00:08:36,431 --> 00:08:40,451
including Crescent Flats and
Meyer Flats on Meyer Road,

129
00:08:40,531 --> 00:08:45,525
were withdrawn because the bids
did not reach their reserve prices.

130
00:08:45,525 --> 00:08:49,625
Belle Vue itself became
the subject of a dispute.

131
00:08:49,625 --> 00:08:53,682
In 1956 the house was
classified as an "ancient

132
00:08:53,762 --> 00:08:57,900
monument" under the
colony's draft Master Plan.

133
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:01,373
The estate's trustees
objected that the label would

134
00:09:01,453 --> 00:09:04,647
cut its resale value,
while a senior government

135
00:09:04,727 --> 00:09:08,339
architect argued that it
was one of the few surviving

136
00:09:08,419 --> 00:09:12,250
examples of an 1840s planter's
home and should be kept.

137
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:15,025
The house no longer stands.

138
00:09:15,025 --> 00:09:19,110
Meyer Mansions was demolished
in 1970 to make way

139
00:09:19,190 --> 00:09:23,615
for Peninsula Plaza, the
Adelphi Hotel closed in 1973

140
00:09:23,695 --> 00:09:27,610
and was pulled down in
1980, and Crescent Flats

141
00:09:27,690 --> 00:09:32,025
and Meyer Flats were
demolished in the early 1990s.

142
00:09:32,025 --> 00:09:34,500
The synagogues survived.

143
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:39,019
Maghain Aboth was gazetted
as a national monument on

144
00:09:39,099 --> 00:09:45,122
the 27th of February 1998 and
Chesed-El on the 18th of December 1998,

145
00:09:45,202 --> 00:09:48,475
and both are still used for services.

146
00:09:48,475 --> 00:09:53,845
Beside Maghain Aboth, the Jacob
Ballas Centre opened in 2007,

147
00:09:53,925 --> 00:09:58,750
and the Jews of Singapore
Museum opened there in 2021.

148
00:09:58,750 --> 00:10:04,075
His gift to Raffles College is
still visible on Bukit Timah Road.

149
00:10:04,075 --> 00:10:08,238
The Manasseh Meyer Building
was one of six former Raffles

150
00:10:08,318 --> 00:10:12,556
College buildings gazetted
together as a national monument

151
00:10:12,636 --> 00:10:16,352
on the 11th of November
2009, and it is now part of

152
00:10:16,432 --> 00:10:20,750
the National University of
Singapore's Bukit Timah campus.

153
00:10:20,750 --> 00:10:24,661
The trust he set up, now the
Sir Manasseh Meyer Trust,

154
00:10:24,741 --> 00:10:27,475
still supports education and charity.

155
00:10:27,475 --> 00:10:31,738
In 1996 a small Jewish
nursery opened at Oxley Rise;

156
00:10:31,818 --> 00:10:37,334
it grew into a school named after
him and in 2016 moved to a campus

157
00:10:37,414 --> 00:10:42,425
in Sembawang as the Sir Manasseh
Meyer International School.

158
00:10:42,425 --> 00:10:46,431
Singapore's Jewish community,
which was down to a

159
00:10:46,511 --> 00:10:49,516
few hundred people by the late 1960s,

160
00:10:49,596 --> 00:10:54,850
now numbers about 2,500, according
to the Jewish Welfare Board.

161
00:10:54,850 --> 00:10:58,471
Why it matters today: Manasseh
Meyer's story shows how a

162
00:10:58,551 --> 00:11:02,503
trading fortune could be turned
into land, and how land could

163
00:11:02,583 --> 00:11:06,350
be turned into things that
last longer than any building.

164
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:10,497
Almost everything he owned in
town was sold or demolished within

165
00:11:10,577 --> 00:11:14,989
a few decades of his death, but the
synagogues, the college building

166
00:11:15,069 --> 00:11:18,688
and the school he paid for
or inspired are still in use,

167
00:11:18,768 --> 00:11:23,325
and the community he led is larger
today than it was in his lifetime.
